<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kachin people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jingpo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kachin are a group of related peoples of the hills of northern Myanmar and neighbouring parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, the largest of them the Jingpo. Traditionally living by swidden farming under hereditary chiefs, they long interacted with the Shan princes of the valleys. Under British rule many became Christians, and since independence the Kachin Independence Organisation has fought the Burmese government for autonomy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kachin-people.jpg" alt="Kachin people" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yoav David · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kachin_women_in_traditional_dress.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>British Burma was governed as a province of India until 1937, with the central plains under direct administration and the hill areas of the Shan, <b>Kachin</b>, Chin and Karenni peoples ruled separately through their own chiefs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/myanmar">Myanmar · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Hsenwi's saophas ruled a large territory of Shan, <b>Kachin</b>, Palaung and Wa peoples, trading tea, cotton and silver along the caravan routes to China.</blockquote><a href="/state/hsenwi-state">Hsenwi State</a></li><li><blockquote>Its saopha ruled over Shan, <b>Kachin</b> and other upland peoples, drawing wealth from rice valleys and from the jade mines of Hpakant, which supplied Chinese markets.</blockquote><a href="/state/mogaung">Mogaung</a></li><li><blockquote>Its saophas ruled a mix of Shan, <b>Kachin</b> and Burman subjects and were among the most aggressive of the Shan rulers.</blockquote><a href="/state/mohnyin">Mohnyin</a></li><li><blockquote>Its saopha ruled valleys of rice and tea worked by Shan, Palaung and <b>Kachin peoples</b>, under the overlordship first of Ava and later of the Toungoo and Konbaung kings.</blockquote><a href="/state/momeik-state">Momeik State</a></li><li><blockquote>The saopha of North Hsenwi governed a large and ethnically mixed territory of Shan, <b>Kachin</b>, Palaung, Chinese and Wa peoples, with trade routes to China.</blockquote><a href="/state/north-hsenwi">North Hsenwi</a></li></ul></section>
